"false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463910} {:answer/id #uuid "fc23aaf2-cd82-5980-a662-d5c724c0a3ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4af26650-ec92-5825-8bf7-ee6389246739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463910} :answer-field/id #uuid "26cc8fec-a525-553b-84c7-2a49f9f7a723" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}+y=x,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464889} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8050b864-d756-570c-bd55-2d6a32259063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463912} {:answer/id #uuid "15e5f25d-f44f-5e9b-a0ce-a0a2af2176a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15e5f25d-f44f-5e9b-a0ce-a0a2af2176a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463913} :answer-field/id #uuid "caf4a2dd-ef1c-5f26-ad2d-413c7be0f7cc" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}$ $=xy,$ $\\,y(0)$ $=3,$ $\\,y(1)$ $=4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464890} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f248fc35-c661-5a21-bea0-6e5c93c92540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463914} {:answer/id #uuid "639ef54f-38f0-5daa-b3e3-33ab6892a11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "639ef54f-38f0-5daa-b3e3-33ab6892a11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463915} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b143b8-d1db-5fb6-b810-dd1742976cc5" :answer-field/key "$2{y}^{″}+{e}^{x}{y}^{′}$ $=0,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(1)$ $=0,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(2)$ $=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e2ad896-b5d8-56df-9f2a-960d9cc29ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130997" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. The differential equation is combined with two conditions, but both conditions are given at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$). This is an **initial value problem.**\n\n- The second system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=1$).\n\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$)."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050677 :topic/id #uuid "5e132315-9606-5b30-bee3-61f0209d40c4" :topic/math-academy-id 2548 :topic/title "Introduction to Second-Order Linear ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6072c7e5-1b9e-53ce-b826-0c4a3ff90a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463916} {:answer/id #uuid "12f38159-dc63-5fba-849a-fd4cbddd37a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6072c7e5-1b9e-53ce-b826-0c4a3ff90a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463916} :answer-field/id #uuid "e220417c-1d48-5050-a73f-0084d4f70153" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+y$ $=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(1)$ $=0,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(2)$ $=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464892} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f55f435-e3f7-5996-93ac-f561dbe5c765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463918} {:answer/id #uuid "b56b3f3f-19de-51b4-b8a9-fc77eab7606e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b56b3f3f-19de-51b4-b8a9-fc77eab7606e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463919} :answer-field/id #uuid "69952d04-741c-5c07-a22e-2cc948d39226" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y$ $=1,$ $\\,y(\\pi )$ $=1,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )$ $=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464893} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad3b12fe-cd05-5467-8f10-72a36bc176df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463920} {:answer/id #uuid "fbdfa32f-e041-5523-8c32-8b0bb4b269a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad3b12fe-cd05-5467-8f10-72a36bc176df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463920} :answer-field/id #uuid "6309537b-a09e-5bac-8903-1a1e4188fddb" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=x{e}^{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d793473-d655-59e4-94f6-f213bc81d597" :question/math-academy-id "q-131001" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=\\pi $).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050032 :topic/id #uuid "277bf882-73ab-515e-84e5-4f7920acb235" :topic/math-academy-id 2549 :topic/title "The Superposition Principle"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be623d32-4c83-52ca-b054-8ae804533930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463922} {:answer/id #uuid "197b7b8b-1618-51ee-a1d0-602649a1d395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be623d32-4c83-52ca-b054-8ae804533930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463922} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb7da93f-871b-59ab-a1ff-a6153daafe12" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-3x}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464895} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4fe36c6-91c9-5794-a277-8e54026a0a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463924} {:answer/id #uuid "484fb7b2-afe2-55df-b44e-37f39f4d84eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "484fb7b2-afe2-55df-b44e-37f39f4d84eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463925} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ea33b57-7f98-5328-978c-c61dfdbb78dd" :answer-field/key "$y(x)={e}^{4x}+{e}^{-3x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f14051-6183-5864-b2e8-96c6ef2d3590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463926} {:answer/id #uuid "9443aa51-acd9-5ce5-a003-657c72a87b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17f14051-6183-5864-b2e8-96c6ef2d3590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463926} :answer-field/id #uuid "2393e142-424a-5c02-bddc-5ad48eec1ba4" :answer-field/key "$y(x)={e}^{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bddcd08-8ec0-5aef-80c9-79dc52612a17" :question/math-academy-id "q-131013" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{4x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-12y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-3x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{4x}+{e}^{-3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}={e}^{4x}\\cdot {e}^{-3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)={e}^{4x}+{e}^{-3x}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050032 :topic/id #uuid "277bf882-73ab-515e-84e5-4f7920acb235" :topic/math-academy-id 2549 :topic/title "The Superposition Principle"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16df337d-8576-522d-8836-284d6911bacf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463928} {:answer/id #uuid "500ed30b-205a-5c4c-8211-d17d01a58518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16df337d-8576-522d-8836-284d6911bacf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463928} :answer-field/id #uuid "60523086-050b-5f92-b28e-ecaa414aceb6" :answer-field/key "$y(x)={x}^{6}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464898} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6adb727-0baa-5326-8e66-de05be9f2eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463930} {:answer/id #uuid "fe9202e9-0898-5199-adda-5e295b71acac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6adb727-0baa-5326-8e66-de05be9f2eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463930} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a2c9a13-7077-5361-9bf5-4c60e322dc18" :answer-field/key "$y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464899} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9a4e554-a8e4-5d20-be63-024b22e44187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463932} {:answer/id #uuid "18a51eb7-83a7-5210-8fa3-f79f5466c0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18a51eb7-83a7-5210-8fa3-f79f5466c0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463933} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e88fe49-bfc9-5250-9ea4-94b933fcaf66" :answer-field/key "$y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df1f63a2-8822-5dfd-aa6c-f1c9c0adcac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-131014" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{6}={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $4x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})={x}^{2}+{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050032 :topic/id #uuid "277bf882-73ab-515e-84e5-4f7920acb235" :topic/math-academy-id 2549 :topic/title "The Superposition Principle"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32f78c56-298b-5849-b85e-66ae8c5979f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463934} {:answer/id #uuid "05e27b72-6ce3-5b06-bdb8-a643f37319c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32f78c56-298b-5849-b85e-66ae8c5979f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463934} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3cf08ac-c38c-5451-a216-7bc713a6faf8" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464901} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5234c90-ac66-525b-ac97-39c9db5c2b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463936} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb0694b-b963-5e9a-a6ab-ca887d89c228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cb0694b-b963-5e9a-a6ab-ca887d89c228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463937} :answer-field/id #uuid "01b6f350-23a3-5cf7-872e-2fb6e4d91e49" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=\\sqrt{x}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464902} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de652c36-7ad1-5a65-86fb-b7cceb87bf14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463938} {:answer/id #uuid "33c401e5-019a-5b4f-bc01-12134fc0618e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de652c36-7ad1-5a65-86fb-b7cceb87bf14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463938} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb896600-7a23-5819-9363-f8b7843a99b5" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a36da13-eb76-5bc5-81fc-b75234c7173d" :question/math-academy-id "q-131016" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ are solutions to the equation $4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050032 :topic/id #uuid "277bf882-73ab-515e-84e5-4f7920acb235" :topic/math-academy-id 2549 :topic/title "The Superposition Principle"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0376114-c41b-5238-8817-841eba5d5fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463940} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae21342-124b-5d16-ac26-43ea0aade282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0376114-c41b-5238-8817-841eba5d5fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463940} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c8c015-0913-5d4b-8128-2ce7e9902635" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464904} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b29eef7-0dba-5507-bb77-8d6b6d46d8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463942} {:answer/id #uuid "7144041a-6509-5887-8f83-1d43e7ff6e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b29eef7-0dba-5507-bb77-8d6b6d46d8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463942} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d17a32d-ae79-505d-9bc9-6757a44babdf" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=x{e}^{2x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464905} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f3fa1d4-4501-591a-8efe-d15597443e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463944} {:answer/id #uuid "072a40d8-5171-509c-8c5c-478de2c5e722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "072a40d8-5171-509c-8c5c-478de2c5e722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463945} :answer-field/id #uuid "733e640b-eaa7-5cd7-9bc6-43cfad37e79b" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=(1+x){e}^{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fca8233-9802-5cda-93e4-fba4e6ea4800" :question/math-academy-id "q-131019" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x=\\frac{x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=x{e}^{2x}=x{e}^{x}({e}^{x})$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.  The superposition principle does not extend to the product of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=(1+x){e}^{x}={e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)=(1+x){e}^{x}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050032 :topic/id #uuid "277bf882-73ab-515e-84e5-4f7920acb235" :topic/math-academy-id 2549 :topic/title "The Superposition Principle"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89cdd4b7-c024-51bc-adbd-f4aab072d58e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463946} {:answer/id #uuid "60b58984-556a-5ebc-8310-43277b466639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60b58984-556a-5ebc-8310-43277b466639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463947} :answer-field/id #uuid "398167ef-5d15-5d48-8733-5686bd40682d" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464907} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eed794f-3430-57c1-aed4-d1c5c1cdbdc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463948} {:answer/id #uuid "6fecefa9-5dae-5c84-84e8-5a80a9d44297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fecefa9-5dae-5c84-84e8-5a80a9d44297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463949} :answer-field/id #uuid "824bd561-b0e8-51d8-903d-d027895758a9" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464908} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfe9e923-204d-591b-bf56-ff2d28af2e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463950} {:answer/id #uuid "115ea48b-81c6-5024-9814-da5ac2474e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfe9e923-204d-591b-bf56-ff2d28af2e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463950} :answer-field/id #uuid "9301f225-85e3-5553-ba4b-22535d83581f" :answer-field/key "$y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d36ff0c1-6dbb-5187-9838-81e30505ca16" :question/math-academy-id "q-131020" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-3.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$; $y(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050032 :topic/id #uuid "277bf882-73ab-515e-84e5-4f7920acb235" :topic/math-academy-id 2549 :topic/title "The Superposition Principle"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d468c753-db8b-58e4-87c3-df3ac00a8458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463952} {:answer/id #uuid "f95137a9-927e-5b87-8152-5572d7b72ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f95137a9-927e-5b87-8152-5572d7b72ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463953} :answer-field/id #uuid "f174bfe1-f553-5267-bcd8-ba2baea7f21b" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464910} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17459f1a-8c7e-5300-a88d-ab46293a02f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463954} {:answer/id #uuid "30ca409d-f9ad-5813-a9e3-c7cc11b108a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17459f1a-8c7e-5300-a88d-ab46293a02f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463954} :answer-field/id #uuid "239cb443-f2fd-5fe0-bf08-925411d1f0db" :answer-field/key "$y(x)={x}^{4}\\sqrt{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464911} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7202512b-f3b0-5c77-bd36-c9e8422bba02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463956} {:answer/id #uuid "25557e74-5aaf-5782-8d90-734f868585fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7202512b-f3b0-5c77-bd36-c9e8422bba02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463956} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d77270c-5036-597e-844c-148993fff4a1" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{e}^{x}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfa49172-fd9a-5f39-9f7a-58af40e909ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-131022" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{4}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-7x{y}^{′}+16y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{4}\\sqrt{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{e}^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotient of a known solution and a non-constant function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050032 :topic/id #uuid "277bf882-73ab-515e-84e5-4f7920acb235" :topic/math-academy-id 2549 :topic/title "The Superposition Principle"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f14b3700-b849-579c-8237-42f5738ae818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463958} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd72fca-5ab9-5351-8970-be26fa6994f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cd72fca-5ab9-5351-8970-be26fa6994f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463959} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eaf6d1d-76e4-5aa5-9a3c-e8fe5c692963" :answer-field/key "$y(x)={e}^{x}+4{e}^{-x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464913} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92b4a50d-43e1-5e28-9457-a4e61f16d918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463960} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5762e5-dbcd-5e74-93f6-4a794c5ce077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e5762e5-dbcd-5e74-93f6-4a794c5ce077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463961} :answer-field/id #uuid "36cd8da3-3a96-5875-98a4-8d7339547ad3" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=5{e}^{-x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464914} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bfea326-4a9a-5f86-bd7d-d79f16288b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463962} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd86b8f-58db-594c-b0aa-a89dd46e4ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bfea326-4a9a-5f86-bd7d-d79f16288b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463962} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fb16613-6593-5ae6-ac15-64bcf45109ac" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=({e}^{-x}+1){e}^{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55d7974d-e6ac-57d3-85a4-a96c054848c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-131036" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{e}^{x}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}+4{e}^{-x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=1,{c}_{2}=4.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=5{e}^{-x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=0,{c}_{2}=5.$\n\n- The function $({e}^{-x}+1){e}^{x}=1+{e}^{x}$ is **not** a solution of the given equation because $1$ is not a solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571 :topic/title "The Extreme Value Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53a22dd3-a12d-5858-8abe-50ea411d89b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463964} {:answer/id #uuid "36046449-1599-5cbe-a388-6f9f7a5cb79c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53a22dd3-a12d-5858-8abe-50ea411d89b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463964} :answer-field/id #uuid "d07414d7-38bb-5a41-a70a-cab1a7c6266e" :answer-field/key "$[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464916} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ef93042-d8eb-5786-ae85-d1a2674d5ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463966} {:answer/id #uuid "7809c3f4-194e-508e-aa57-63f005ea71e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7809c3f4-194e-508e-aa57-63f005ea71e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463967} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0621149-4a49-5264-943b-8b19e5114ba8" :answer-field/key "$[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464917} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cead2490-5a8f-5617-b718-96a31861ea17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463968} {:answer/id #uuid "44cc7066-d7c7-5444-8303-dac86b7c407b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cead2490-5a8f-5617-b718-96a31861ea17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463968} :answer-field/id #uuid "372a4dc1-c392-5350-a730-58b07fa01c7e" :answer-field/key "$[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d91387a3-da34-5d28-af8d-36338cffabf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-131066" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2, & x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}2=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function is not defined at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571 :topic/title "The Extreme Value Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ead1ab53-2f38-562e-b56e-9a29a4a4568d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463970} {:answer/id #uuid "3665607f-b7d9-5123-b9f7-32fbfd2a28e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ead1ab53-2f38-562e-b56e-9a29a4a4568d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463970} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5b9e58b-7eab-5359-b29e-a7937b659878" :answer-field/key "$[0,2]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464919} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b0a6304-df8f-57bc-a2be-8e3c2fe89bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463972} {:answer/id #uuid "818dbaff-1ecd-5434-8737-c9a200f406ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "818dbaff-1ecd-5434-8737-c9a200f406ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463973} :answer-field/id #uuid "20fb51a7-1b3c-5f04-95a7-b29b663634ac" :answer-field/key "$[2,4]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464920} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70c3be20-0c83-52a6-9944-b8343279455d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463974} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ed2a08-9769-59a3-bded-911458d59cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70c3be20-0c83-52a6-9944-b8343279455d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463974} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ffc2b5e-f24f-5354-9fb7-9a6e56b3ffa5" :answer-field/key "$[0,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f0ecd5f3-9f3c-5ba8-8557-dd384f7804ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-131068" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(2x)=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[2,4].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,4].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[0,1)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[2,4]$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571 :topic/title "The Extreme Value Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5197832f-c920-574d-8c49-3f63005d0fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463976} {:answer/id #uuid "be0c064d-6c98-5b3b-b65b-a4b7d2be0a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 